  "body": "Stacked on PR 1464 (base `issue/1456-solidcraft-import`); retarget to `main` before 1464 merges.\n\n## What changed\n- `camcraft_kernel::solidcraft::MAX_COORD_MM = 1e6` mm. After `native::decode`, the reader checks every vertex of the final body: a non-finite coordinate or one past ±1e6 mm is refused by name (\"the final body's vertex i lies at ... past the 1e6 mm coordinate limit\"). Before this, only the decoder's own bound (1e12) applied.\n- New test binary `crates/kernel/tests/solidcraft_hostile.rs`. It splices edited or damaged bodies into the sample and fixes up the slice table, both SHA-256 digests and the CRC-32s, so each case reaches the body decoder:\n  - `hostile_coordinates_are_refused_by_name`: covers 1e9, just past the limit, -5e7, and NaN/±inf/1e13 patched over a sentinel's f64 bytes. Each is refused, naming the final body.\n  - `damaged_bodies_never_panic_or_hang`: 24 bodies with random byte flips, 0xFFFFFFFF length words or truncation, run under a 120 s bound. Every refusal must name the final body. Result: 0 panics, and 6 still import because the damage landed in metadata.\n  - `a_deflate_entry_past_its_declared_size_is_cut_off`: 64 MiB of deflate output declared as 64 bytes is refused with \"65 bytes, the directory says 64\".\n- Decision record: `docs/decisions/1467-sc-reader-hardening.md` (why 1 km).\n\n## Tests\n`cargo test -p camcraft-kernel --test solidcraft_hostile --test solidcraft_import`: 3 + 7 pass (Windows, debug, about 40 s each). `cargo clippy -p camcraft-kernel --all-targets -- -D warnings`: clean.\n\n## Not exercised\n- Vertex tolerance is not checked again, because the record's validator already refuses anything outside (0, 1e3].\n- For 1e13 the decoder's wording is \"non-finite metadata\", which is solidcore's text, not ours.\n- No Linux or macOS run.\n\nChangelog: Solidcraft `.sc` import refuses a final body with a vertex farther than 1 km from the origin, by name (#1467)\n\nEffort: high\n\nFixes #1467\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n",
